The sum is 7 plus die 3, so it settles between 8 and 13, with a fair value of 10.5.

Quoting the ask at 13.0 means an informed trader never buys, since its valuation can be at most 13 and it must exceed my ask. A noise trader buys with probability about 0.20, giving an edge of 2.5 on that trade. That is about 0.33 expected profit, which is better than nearby ask prices. By symmetry, the best bid is 8.0.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 13.0}

Die 1 came up 6, so the contract settles at 6 + die 2 + die 3, which starts at a mean of 13. The buy at my 15.4 ask suggests a higher value. An informed buyer only does that if the sum is 16 or more, which happens 1/6 of the time. Weighting the possible outcomes by how likely each makes that buy raises fair value to about **13.6**.

I'm keeping a wide spread of about 2.3 on each side around 13.6. Noise traders' valuations vary by about 3, so I still get trades, and the spread covers adverse selection from informed traders. My position is flat, so I don't need to lean the quote either way.

{"bid": 11.3, "ask": 15.9}

Expected settlement is 19.6, the sum of the three die means: 7 for die 1, 4.6 for die 2 and 8 for die 3. The standard deviation of the sum is about 3.6.

I tested half-spreads of 2.5 to 4 against a normal approximation. The gain from noise traders minus the loss to informed traders peaks at about 0.27 per side near a half-spread of 3.5. So I quote 19.6 ± 3.5.

{"bid": 16.1, "ask": 23.1}

Fair value is the sum of the three dice means: 8 + 5.33 + 6.5 = 19.83. The standard deviation of the sum is about 5.7.

I compared the expected profit at different half-spreads. Wider quotes earn more per noise trade but fill less often, while narrower quotes lose more to informed traders. The expected profit per quote peaks at a half-spread of about 6. So I quote roughly 19.8 ± 6.

{"bid": 13.8, "ask": 25.9}

Fair value is 21, the expected sum of three cards. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.35, nearly the same as the noise traders' 6.4. The last quote got no trade, and since my quote was symmetric around 21, that tells us nothing either way. Fair value stays at 21.

I set the half-spread to balance two things: the profit I make from noise traders and the losses I take from informed traders. When both groups' valuations have a similar spread, the best half-spread is about 1.19 standard deviations, roughly 7.6. That gives a bid of 13.4 and an ask of 28.6.

{"bid": 13.4, "ask": 28.6}

Fair value is still 21, since each card averages 7. The sum has a standard deviation of about 6.35. Last quote's no-trade carries little information and is symmetric, so fair stays 21.

To pick the half-spread, I weighed the profit from noise traders against expected losses to informed traders, who arrive 35% of the time. Expected profit per side peaks at a half-spread of about 5 to 6 points. I'm keeping roughly the same width, slightly tighter at 5 points each side.

{"bid": 16.0, "ask": 26.0}

The expected sum is 3 × 7 = 21, since a single card averages 7. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.35: one card has variance 14, so three cards drawn without replacement give 3 × 14 × 49/51 ≈ 40.4.

I weighed the profit from noise traders against the loss to informed traders at different half-spreads. The expected net profit per side peaks at a half-spread of about 5 to 6, at roughly +0.21 per side. I'll quote symmetrically around 21 with a half-spread of 5.5.

{"bid": 15.5, "ask": 26.5}

Fair value: cards 1 and 2 sum to 20. Card 3 is drawn from the 50 cards left, so its expected value is (364 − 8 − 12)/50 = 6.88. That puts fair value near 26.88, with settlement somewhere from 21 to 33.

For each side I weighed the expected loss to an informed trader against the expected gain from noise traders. The best ask is about 31.5, worth about +0.45 per quote. The best bid is about 22.5, also worth about +0.46. My position of −2 leaves room to trade on either side, and holding inventory doesn't change the expected profit.

{"bid": 22.5, "ask": 31.5}

Card 1 is a King (13). The remaining 51 cards sum to 364 − 13 = 351, an average of about 6.88 each. Expected settlement is therefore 13 + 2 × 6.88 ≈ 26.8.

The stage‑1 trades roughly cancel out. A buy at 27.4 and a sell at 20 can't both come from informed traders, so they tell me little. I'll centre near 26.8 with a fairly wide spread. The sum of the two unknown cards has a standard deviation of about 5.2, so informed traders have a real edge.

{"bid": 22.7, "ask": 30.9}

Expected sum: the deck totals 119 across 15 cards, so each card averages about 7.93, and three cards average about 23.8.

Spread: the sum's standard deviation is about 9.4. That is wider than the noise traders' spread of 7, so informed traders would pick off a tight quote. A rough calculation puts the break-even half-spread at about 10–12, so I'll quote wide around 23.8.

{"bid": 13, "ask": 34}

The expected value of one card is 118/13 ≈ 9.08, so the expected sum of three cards is about 27.2. Because the cards are dealt without replacement, the standard deviation of the sum is about 7.8.

Informed traders know the exact sum, and that sum can be far from 27.2. I therefore quote a fairly wide spread of about ±5 around fair value. That spread still lets me earn from noise traders, whose valuations spread around 27.2 with a standard deviation of 5.8.

{"bid": 22.4, "ask": 32.0}

Card 1 is a 2, the lowest card in the deck, so the settlement is exactly min(2, card 2) = 2. There is no risk left, and an informed trader (valuation exactly 2) won't trade against a symmetric quote around 2.

Noise traders value the contract at 2 + N(0, 6.3). The expected profit per side is x·P(noise > x), which is highest at about x ≈ 0.75σ ≈ 4.7. That gives a bid of -2.7 and an ask of 6.7. Being long 1 doesn't matter, because the value is certain.

{"bid": -2.7, "ask": 6.7}

Card 1 is a 15, the highest card in the deck, so the contract settles at exactly 15. My quote only needs to make money against noise traders. Their valuations are spread around 15 with a standard deviation of 2.7, and an informed trader valuing at exactly 15 will not trade with me.

The edge per trade is the distance d from 15, times the chance a noise trader crosses. That product, d·Φ(−d/2.7), is largest at about d ≈ 2. So I quote symmetrically about 15 at 13 and 17. My position is −2, so I can still buy up to 5 units and sell 1, and both sides are allowed.

{"bid": 13.0, "ask": 17.0}

The 7 revealed flips show 5 heads and 2 tails. That gives about a 52.9% chance the coin is the biased one, so P(flip 8 is heads) ≈ 0.659. The settlement is therefore 25 or 30, and the fair value is about 28.3.

An informed trader values the contract at either 25 or 30. A bid at or below 25 and an ask at or above 30 means I never trade with them at a loss. Within those limits I set each price to maximise expected profit from noise traders, whose valuations are spread around 28.3 with SD 6.6. That gives a bid of about 23.5 and an ask of about 33.

{"bid": 23.5, "ask": 33}

Revealed flips (0,1,1) imply a settlement of about 45. The two buys at the ask (57.4 and 59), though, are strong evidence that an informed trader knows the value is 60, which is the maximum possible. Updating on those trades puts the chance of 60 at about 80% and the fair value at about 56.

I quote the ask above 60 so that no informed trader can ever buy from me. I quote the bid well below fair value, so that sales from noise traders outweigh the losses to informed sellers when the value is low.

{"bid": 49, "ask": 61}

Fair value now is 16.8. The chance of heads is 0.56 (0.7×0.5 + 0.3×0.7), so expected heads over 6 flips is 3.36, and 3.36 × 5 = 16.8.

The settlement value has a standard deviation of about 6.6. Informed traders can pick off a tight quote, while noise traders are spread wider (standard deviation 9.9). So I quote roughly 4.2 either side of fair value.

{"bid": 12.6, "ask": 21.0}

Public fair value is about 8.35. After two red draws, the probability of each red-ball count R is proportional to R(R−1), which gives an expected value of 8.0. The buy at 8.9 on quote 7 raises this a little, because an informed buyer would only buy there if R is 9 or 10.

I'm long 2, so I skew slightly toward selling. An ask at 9.0 is only bought by an informed trader when R = 10, which caps that loss at 1. A bid at 7.2 sits a bit wider than the ask side because I'd rather not add to my long position.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 9.0}

Given two reds and one blue drawn, each possible red count r has weight proportional to r(r-1)(10-r). That puts the posterior mean of the red count at about **6.2**.

I'm long 3, so I can only quote an ask. Weighing what noise traders pay against losses to informed traders when the true count is 8 or 9, expected edge peaks for an ask around 8.0 to 8.2.

{"bid": null, "ask": 8.1}

Posterior fair value is about 38. After the draws 0, 0, 1, the posterior on R (the number of red balls) is proportional to R(10−R)(9−R). That gives E[R] = 3.8, so fair value is 38, with a standard deviation of about 18.

The 0.8 chance of noise traders (sd 47) makes a fairly wide spread pay best. Informed traders cost little at those widths, because the value is unlikely to be far from 38. I'm short 1, so I'll skew the quotes slightly upward.

{"bid": 18, "ask": 60}

Weighing the reveals and the trade history, I put fair value near 15 to 16. The two blue draws leave 9 unseen balls with a prior mean of 2.7 red, which would give 27. But two sells at low bids pull it down: one at 19.9 in stage 1 and one at 10.1 at quote 4. The buy at 20.9 pushes it up.

The posterior has two humps. The most likely case is 1 red ball, a value of about 10, with roughly 55% weight. The other case is 3 or more red, a value of 30 or more. I'm long 1, so I'll quote wide and lean slightly lower.

